WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.219
Why reverse mode, and what it costs.

2
00:00:02.499 --> 00:00:05.229
There are two ways to apply the chain rule mechanically.

3
00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:13.147
Forward mode picks one input and pushes “derivative with respect to this input” forward through the graph alongside the values.

4
00:00:13.427 --> 00:00:18.888
Reverse mode picks one output and pulls “derivative of this output” backward to all inputs.

5
00:00:19.168 --> 00:00:32.928
Baydin and colleagues’ survey of automatic differentiation lays out the trade-off: for a function from R^n to R^m, forward mode needs about n passes to get the full Jacobian and reverse mode needs about m.

6
00:00:33.208 --> 00:00:41.656
Training a network means differentiating a single scalar loss with respect to every parameter, so m = 1 and n can be billions.

7
00:00:41.936 --> 00:00:48.251
One reverse pass gives the whole gradient for a small constant multiple of the cost of the forward pass.

8
00:00:48.531 --> 00:01:00.093
That asymmetry is the entire reason backpropagation, popularized for neural networks by Rumelhart, Hinton, and Williams in 1986, is the workhorse of deep learning.

9
00:01:00.373 --> 00:01:01.675
The price is memory.

10
00:01:01.955 --> 00:01:14.115
Reverse mode needs the forward pass’s intermediate values (here, t inside tanh’s backward, and both operands inside multiply’s), so it keeps them until the backward pass consumes them.

11
00:01:14.395 --> 00:01:19.515
Baydin and colleagues name this storage requirement as the main drawback of reverse mode.

12
00:01:19.795 --> 00:01:30.141
Scaled up to large networks, it is why training needs far more memory than running the same model, a point you’ll hear again whenever people discuss fine-tuning budgets.
